title The clock that spoke one dialect, and the guard that would have said so
prompt Autonomous loop -- pull xykj61 first, roster cold, Standfast on reds, next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first, rota row N mod 5, send each finished increment
think Opened the seated way: git pull --rebase xykj61 main before reading a byte of context. Up to date, so the card read is the tree I stand on.
think Cold roster before touching anything: 66 guards run, 65 green, ONE RED -- readme_reach -- and tree_moved=no. Standfast: that comes before the named crux.
think The red's root is one link. The previous lap lifted REDS row %249 from construction/REDS.md onto construction/archive/REDS-the-gate-that-guarded-nothing-rows-249.md, and the row body cited [../tools/fixtures/shell_portable.sh], correct from construction/ and naming construction/tools/... from construction/archive/. The shelf's own fresh prose spelled the same file ../../tools/... correctly, so the file disagreed with itself. Repaired to ../../tools/; readme_reach GREEN with its control proving both directions. Booked %256.
think Council rota row 3488 mod 5 = 3 -- Water, Venus, care and flow, the sense taste. Read the threshold and all three seats. The cardinal seat (Gall and Conway) shaped the whole repair: grow the working simple thing rather than design a new one, so shell_portable.sh -- which already answers one dialect question -- grew a second family rather than a new file being born beside it. The fixed seat (the happy zone) shaped the proof: two small answers at every seam, the asker proven against the contract and the answerer proven to keep it.
think Rota honesty: the previous lap read row 0 and this lap reads row 3, so rows 1 and 2 went unread. That is exactly the measured fault the seed names -- N counts commits and a lap may make two or none. The proposed fix (advance by lap, read from the previous log) waits on Keaton's word, so the seated reading stands and the skip is recorded rather than quietly corrected.
think REDS %250's remaining half was the named crux: nine date -d sites. Read every one up close before touching any -- the water row's discipline, taste only works up close -- and the nine turned out to be SEVEN distinct lines. pond/apps/corpora/one_clock.rish is a tracked symlink (mode 120000) to tools/o/one_clock_witness.rish, already on the roster, and grep follows it. Booked %257.
think The seven fall into three shapes, and only two of them need a dialect answer at all. Parsing a stamp to epoch: GNU date -d STRING, BSD date -j -f INFMT STRING. Formatting an epoch back: GNU date -d @EPOCH, BSD date -r EPOCH. The third shape, "four hours from now", needs NO extension -- adding seconds to date +%s is arithmetic every host spells alike, and only the formatting afterwards wanted a dialect.
think Order matters in exactly one direction, and it is worth saying why in the code. GNU rejects -j and -v cleanly, so trying BSD first on a GNU host is safe. -r is the trap: BSD reads it as an epoch and GNU reads it as a FILE whose mtime to report, so date -r 1787742895 on GNU searches for a file of that name. It fails today and would answer a wrong time the day such a file exists. GNU therefore goes first in both functions.
think Proved the fault on metal before repairing it, on this pier, without a Mac: built a date that refuses -d and answers -j -f and -r exactly as BSD's does, and ran the real one_clock_provenance_scan.sh under it. Two PROV_OK on GNU, ZERO under the shim, every stamp in the tree called unparsable. That is the macOS bench's PROV_FAIL count=1, reproduced.
think stamp_to_epoch stood byte for byte in one_clock_provenance_scan.sh and one_clock_head_scan.sh -- one rule two files could come to disagree about, in the one place where disagreeing means calling every stamp unparsable. One function now, one call in each.
think ad_walk_sample.sh read age=0d for every artifact under a BSD date, because its fallback answered "now" when the parse failed. A wrong age that looks exactly like a young one, and the sampler's whole purpose is that no verdict past KEEP rests on a low inbound count without its age beside it. Reads the true age on both dialects now.
think The printed loop recipe carries a wall-clock deadline the operator is explicitly told to edit, so a duration would have changed what they were promised. It carries both spellings side by side instead, and loop_prompt_parse_control.sh's plant follows it -- the plant's value is being a byte-faithful copy of the recipe it measures, so it moves when the recipe moves.
think Promoted the family from advisory to GATED at zero. The scan's own comment says an advisory earns its ceiling by costing a real reading on a real host, and this one has. Two files hold the elder spellings on purpose and are read past: the scan spells every pattern it hunts, and shell_portable.sh spells the GNU leg of each fallback -- that leg IS the repair on a GNU host. A line spelling both dialects is read past for the same reason at a smaller grain.
think Then the finding that matters most. tools/o/one_clock_witness.rish is called blocking by the one-clock law in .claude/rules/session-logs.md and by the EDT host convention spec, and it stood on NONE of the sixty-nine roster rows. No lap ran it -- cold, hot, or cadence. That is why the clock half of %250 went unseen on this pier for a day and had to be reported by the bench that ran it by hand. REDS %219's class, reaching the guard for the tree's most-cited law. Booked %258 and seated one_clock on the every-lap tier, roster 69 -> 70.
obs A repair to a guard is finished when the guard runs. Three of this round's four moves were the repair and the fourth was the schedule, and without the fourth the first three would have been correct and unheard on exactly the pier that needed them.
obs The nine was a number I inherited from the previous lap's card, log, and REDS row, and it was wrong by two. Reading the meter's own site list file by file is what caught it -- two entries naming the same two line numbers under two paths. Measurement beats memory, including the memory of a measurement taken one lap ago.
obs A ceiling of zero has no tree one site under it, so both new ceilings are proven from both sides on one planted tree: the same single planted site refuses at zero and passes at one. A refusal proven only in the passing direction cannot be told from a bypass.
obs The plant in prove-date must first read correctly on GNU before its silence under the shim is allowed to mean anything -- the same vacuum prove-portable already closes. A plant that cannot speak proves nothing by staying quiet.
obs The REDS pin needed two shelves to stay under its bound: %250 CLOSED and %253 CLOSED, each onto its own immutable page, leaving %230, %252, %255 open and %256, %257, %258 fresh. The spine reads 1..258 with no gaps, and the headline was regenerated rather than hand-typed.
obs %256 and %253 are one lesson from two sides: a molt moves the NAME and strands references TO it, a shelf moves the DEPTH and strands references FROM it. Neither is reached by machinery built on the basename.
think The hot roster caught a red of my own, and it is the one the cold roster could never have caught. I taught the new prove-date leg's witness to assert living_bsd_shaped=2, one paragraph after quoting prove-portable's own comment saying to compare readings rather than pin a constant. The provenance scan weighs every dated artifact the working tree AND INDEX hold, so the count went 2 -> 3 the moment this round staged its own session log. Booked %259, repaired to assert the comparison rather than either reading, and proved the refusing side on metal: under a host whose date refuses -d outright the leg answers subject_reads_nothing and exits 1 rather than passing on zero against zero.
obs REDS %174 earned its keep this lap. The fault only exists once an index is dirty, which is precisely the state --hot was built to measure, so a round that ran only the cold roster would have shipped a guard that reds on the next lap that stages anything.
obs docs-geode/libraries/README.md drifted by one row because the round's witness count moved. Regenerated with its own writer rather than by hand, and the guard is GREEN with its four control readings.
obs The REDS pin took a third shelf to stay under bound: %256 and %257 fold together, because they are one sentence -- this tree's reference machinery reads basenames, and neither a shelf's changed depth nor a symlink's second name is a basename. %253 on its own shelf is the third face of the same shape.
obs Held the integration gate again. REDS %248 reserved the two-pier merge for Keaton's word, and main stays unpushed with three packages parked additively behind it.
